      "latency_ms": 2321.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 614,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth.** It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Composition\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms** \n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are held together by strong covalent bonds. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is what makes water such a great solvent (it easily dissolves salts, sugars, and other chemicals).\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature, found in rivers, oceans, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). *Unique fact:* Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Global Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly by oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and shallow aquifers).\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from the surface, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into oceans and rivers.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the medium of life. \n*   The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n*   It acts as a universal solvent, transporting nutrients, minerals, and waste products through the bloodstream and cells.\n*   It regulates body temperature (through sweating and respiration).\n*   It provides the environment in which all the metabolic and chemical reactions of life take place.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the very foundation of weather, geology, and life itself."
